S[8]=dict(lbl="What are proactive work behaviours?|What is new about job crafting?|What is proactive vitality management?|What is playful work design?|How do work and home affect each other?|What can you now answer?",cards=[
Q("Which proactive behaviours does JD-R now include, and how do work and home affect each other?",
 "The first two innovations put the employee in time and context. The last two give the employee more ways to act, and a life outside work. Both connect to the spirals of reading 1 and lead to readings 5 and 6.",
 ["What proactive work behaviours are","Three of them: job crafting, vitality management and playful work design","The work–home resources model"]),
CARD("Self-initiated actions to change your situation or yourself; JD-R now names three.",
 "<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"proactive\">Proactive</b> means acting on your own initiative instead of waiting for the boss:"+UL([("1","<b>Job crafting:</b> change the job."),("2","<b>Proactive vitality management:</b> refuel yourself."),("3","<b>Playful work design:</b> make the task more fun or competitive.")]),
 "memory hook: reshape the job, refuel yourself, play the task.",
 "what is new about crafting."),
CARD("Two insights: optimizing demands works better than reducing them, and crafting can be trained.",
 "Simply <i>reducing</i> demands (avoiding annoying customers, cutting workload) costs more energy than it gives. <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"optdem\">Optimizing demands</b> (simplifying processes, working more efficiently) is linked to more engagement (Demerouti &amp; Peeters, 2018) and performance (Demerouti et al., 2018a). And people can learn job crafting in training (Oprea et al., 2019). <i>Our example:</i> a call-centre agent redesigns her routine to handle standard requests in fewer steps.",
 "an exam option saying reducing demands is the most effective form of crafting is wrong.",
 "changing yourself instead of the job."),
CARD("Actively managing your own energy, for example with a walk in nature or a museum visit, to function well at work.",
 "<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"pvm\">Proactive vitality management</b> changes the self, not the situation. <b>The study:</b> Op den Kamp et al. (2018a). <i>What they found:</i> people were more creative in weeks when they did more of this.",
 "crafting changes the job; vitality management changes the person's own energy.",
 "changing how the task feels."),
CARD("Making the work itself more fun or more competitive, without changing the job.",
 "<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"pwd\">Playful work design</b>: an office worker imagines being Charlie Chaplin while doing emails; a security guard guesses which route a customer will take. <b>The study:</b> Scharp et al. (2022a). <i>What they found:</i> designing fun satisfied the need for relatedness, designing competition the need for competence, and both satisfied the need for autonomy.",
 "play changes the experience of the task, not its content; reading 6 is all about this.",
 "life outside work."),
CARD("Through volatile personal resources such as time, mood and energy, in both directions (the work–home resources model).",
 "The <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"whr\">work–home resources model</b> (ten Brummelhuis &amp; Bakker, 2012): demands and resources at work affect home life through <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"volatile\">volatile personal resources</b>, and home affects work the same way. Key resources (personality, skills) and macro resources (culture, welfare) change how strong these effects are. <b>Study 1:</b> Aw et al. (2021). <i>What they found:</i> helping colleagues gave a sense of accomplishment but also tiredness, especially when help was not returned; tiredness led to withdrawal at home, accomplishment to better family functioning. <b>Study 2:</b> Du et al. (2020). <i>What they found:</i> when employees shared positive events about their children at home the evening before, job demands related positively to next-day task performance.",
 "home life is part of the JD-R story, in both directions.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("Employees can reshape the job, refuel themselves or play the task; and work and home affect each other through time, mood and energy.",
 [("Proactive behaviours?","Self-initiated: crafting, vitality management, playful design."),("Crafting news?","Optimizing beats reducing demands; crafting is trainable."),("Vitality management?","Changing the self; more creativity (Op den Kamp et al., 2018a)."),("Playful design?","Fun → relatedness; competition → competence; both → autonomy."),("Work and home?","Through volatile resources, both ways (ten Brummelhuis & Bakker, 2012).")],
 ["What is the difference between job crafting and proactive vitality management? (situation vs self)","Which statement is wrong? \"Reducing demands is the most effective form of crafting.\"","Name the strategy: a guard turns his rounds into a guessing game (playful work design, competition)"])],
 guides=G("<b>Tap a card</b>: job crafting, vitality management or playful work design.","<b>Tap a part of the bridge</b> between work and home: the volatile resources, the pillars, the sky.","Read the study behind each part."),
 gguide=G("Read what the employee does on the card.","<b>Name the behaviour</b> as the review defines it: a kind of crafting, vitality management, or playful design (fun or competition).","Read why, then go to the next employee."))

S[9]=dict(lbl="Which category mix-ups do researchers make?|What counts as a personal resource?|Are demands always bad and resources always good?|What can organizations do?|What should future research study?|What can you now answer?",cards=[
Q("What mistakes do researchers make with JD-R, what can organizations do, and what is still unknown?",
 "The review ends practically. Table 2 lists common mistakes, then come five routes for organizations and an agenda for research. This is where a work psychologist's job begins: theory and evidence turned into advice at the right level.",
 ["Common mistakes in JD-R research (Table 2)","Five routes for organizations","What research should do next"]),
CARD("Treating a low resource as a demand, or a low demand as a resource.",
 "\"Low support does not mean conflict\", and \"lack of conflict is not support\": the same rule as in reading 1. <i>Our example:</i> a study that codes \"low social support\" as a job demand makes a Table 2 mistake.",
 "demands and resources are separate dials; the low end of one is not the other.",
 "another mix-up: what a personal resource is."),
CARD("Positive beliefs about yourself, not behaviours: so \"proactive coping\" is not a personal resource.",
 "Personal resources are aspects of the self, such as self-efficacy and optimism. Coping is an action. Proactive coping is also not task autonomy, which is a job resource. <i>Our example:</i> \"I believe I can handle this\" is a personal resource; \"I plan ahead for problems\" is a behaviour.",
 "check whether a variable is a belief, a behaviour or a job feature before you label it.",
 "are the categories always good or bad?"),
CARD("No: hindrance demands are detrimental, challenge demands can be both, and resources may even be \"too much of a good thing\".",
 "What matters is testing the two independent processes, health impairment and motivation, with good indicators of each. <i>Our example:</i> so much autonomy that nobody knows what is expected.",
 "do not assume a fixed sign; test both processes.",
 "what organizations can do."),
CARD("Five routes: HR practices, a psychosocial safety climate, job redesign, leader training, and individual interventions.",
 UL([("1","<b>HR practices:</b> open communication, work–life balance, learning."),("2","A <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"psc\">psychosocial safety climate</b>."),("3","<b>Monitoring and redesigning jobs</b>, through participation or team-level job crafting."),("4","<b>Training leaders</b> to offer the right challenges, remove hindrances such as bureaucracy, and enable resource exchange, even with a coffee corner."),("5","<b>Individual interventions</b>, ideally tailored to each person's level of burnout or engagement.")])+"An <b>intervention</b> is a planned action to change something.",
 "four routes are organizational and one is individual; choose the level that fits the problem.",
 "what is still unknown."),
CARD("Interventions at the organizational and team level, team processes such as emotional contagion, and demands and resources from other life domains.",
 "Most interventions so far targeted individuals, so the authors call for more structural, organizational and team-level ones. COVID-19 showed how much other life domains matter. Demerouti and Bakker (2023) propose that resources from one domain can buffer demands from another: workload may be buffered by autonomy at work, but also by autonomy at home.",
 "an exam option saying most JD-R interventions so far targeted teams and organizations is wrong.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("Avoid category mix-ups, test both processes, act at the right level, and study organizational interventions and other life domains.",
 [("Category mix-ups?","Low resource ≠ demand; low demand ≠ resource."),("Personal resources?","Beliefs about the self, not behaviours such as proactive coping."),("Always good or bad?","No: hindrances harm, challenges can do both, resources can be too much."),("What can organizations do?","HR practices, safety climate, job redesign, leader training, tailored individual help."),("Future research?","Organizational and team interventions, contagion, cross-domain buffering.")],
 ["A study calls \"proactive coping\" a personal resource. What is wrong? (coping is a behaviour, not a belief)","Which statement is wrong? \"Most interventions so far targeted teams and organizations.\"","Correct use or Table 2 mistake? (\"low support coded as a demand\" = mistake)"])],
 guides=G("<b>Tap any part</b> of the picture: a mix-up card, a floor of the building, the umbrella, the person or the bars.","Read the explanation for each part.","Use the bars to see where interventions happened so far (direction only, not numbers)."),
 gguide=G("You are reviewing JD-R studies for a journal. Read the study on the card.","Decide: <b>correct use</b> or a <b>Table 2 mistake</b>.","Read why, then go to the next study."))

def run(path):
    s=open(path,encoding='utf-8').read()
    for n,d in S.items():
        s=apply_step(s,W,n,d['lbl'],d['cards'],guides=d.get('guides'),gguide=d.get('gguide'))
    open(path,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s);print('ok',list(S))
if __name__=='__main__': run(sys.argv[1])
